.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I jitterentropy
library provides a source of good entropy by collecting CPU
executing time jitter. The entropy in the CPU execution time
jitter is magnified by the CPU Jitter Random Number Generator.
The CPU Jitter Random Number Generator uses the CPU execution
timing jitter to generate a bit stream which complies with
different statistical measurements that determine the bit
stream is random.
.LP
The CPU Jitter Random Number Generator delivers entropy which
follows information theoretical requirements. Based on these
studies and the implementation, the caller can assume that
one bit of data extracted from the CPU Jitter Random Number
Generator holds one bit of entropy.
.LP
The CPU Jitter Random Number Generator provides a decentralized
source of entropy where the caller does not need to rely
on a centrally maintained source of entropy like
.IR /dev/random
or
.IR /dev/urandom .
.LP
.BR jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl ()
allows the caller to set a thread handler that is used by the
Jitter RNG if it operates in the timer-less mode.
.I jent_notime_init
sets up the handler's context in
.IR *ctx ,
and
.I jent_notime_fini
releases it; it is only invoked with the non-NULL context a successful
.I jent_notime_init
stored.
.I jent_notime_start
starts a thread running
.I start_routine
with
.IR arg ,
as the trailing two parameters of
.BR pthread_create (3)
do, and
.I jent_notime_stop
stops it; it is called exactly once for every start that succeeded. The
Jitter RNG starts and stops the thread frequently.
.IR start_routine 's
type is that of
.BR pthread_create (3)
where the library uses POSIX threads, and
.I "int (*)(void *)"
otherwise.
.BR jent_notime_init ()
and
.BR jent_notime_fini ()
are the library's own implementations of the first two, exported so that a
handler can reuse them rather than duplicate them.
.BR jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl ()
must be called before
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
as after this call, the change of the thread handler is denied.
.LP
.BR jent_entropy_set_notime_cpu ()
pins the counting thread of the timer-less mode to the logical CPU index
.IR cpu .
When it is not set, that thread runs on the highest-numbered CPU in the
affinity set of the thread that starts it - the caller's, which a cpuset or
job object may confine; outside Linux and Windows, where no affinity set can
be read, on the CPU count minus one. As with the thread handler above, it must be called before
.BR jent_entropy_init ():
afterwards the call has no effect and returns
.IR \-EAGAIN .
The pinning is advisory - an index the platform cannot honor, and a platform
with no thread-to-CPU affinity API at all (OpenBSD, macOS on Apple Silicon),
leave the counting thread where the scheduler puts it rather than failing the
call. A CPU outside the process's affinity mask or cpuset is one such index.
On Windows the index counts the active processors of all processor groups in
group order, and a CPU in a processor group other than the counting thread's
is not honored either. It returns
.IR 0
on success and a negative errno otherwise.
.LP
Without the internal timer compiled in, both functions above return
.IR \-EOPNOTSUPP .
.LP
.BR jent_set_fips_failure_callback ()
allows the caller to set a callback that is invoked by the
Jitter RNG when a health test failure is detected. The callback
specified with the parameter
.BR cb
to the API function receives the Jitter RNG state with the parameter
.BR ec
and the FIPS health test failure with the parameter
.BR health_failure ,
a bit mask of
.B JENT_RCT_FAILURE
(1),
.B JENT_APT_FAILURE
(2),
.B JENT_LAG_FAILURE
(4) and
.B JENT_RCT_MEM_FAILURE
(8) for an intermittent failure, and the same bits shifted by
.B JENT_PERMANENT_FAILURE_SHIFT
(16) for a permanent one:
.BR JENT_RCT_FAILURE_PERMANENT ,
.BR JENT_APT_FAILURE_PERMANENT ,
.B JENT_LAG_FAILURE_PERMANENT
and
.BR JENT_RCT_MEM_FAILURE_PERMANENT ,
formed with
.BR JENT_PERMANENT_FAILURE ().
It is invoked for the caller's own instances in FIPS mode, never for the
timer tests of
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
and
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex (),
once per newly raised failure bit, with every bit then standing. It is invoked
wherever the health tests of such an instance are checked: during generation,
and also during the startup entropy collection that every
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ()
and every recovery of
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
runs on the new collector - where an intermittent failure makes the startup
replace that collector in turn. The instance it is handed carries the
identifier
.RB ( jent_uuid ())
and the output totals of the instance the caller holds in each of these
cases, including a replacement still in its startup.
The
.I ec
it is handed is valid for the duration of the call only: a failure may be
followed by a reallocation, which frees it moments later, and during a startup
the collector is not yet one the caller holds. Anything the callback needs
afterwards - the identifier, a status document - has to be copied out while
it runs, not kept as a pointer.
This function must be called before
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
as after this call, the change of the callback is denied.
.LP
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
initializes the CPU Jitter Random Number Generator. The function
performs statistical tests to verify that the underlying system
offers the properties needed for measuring and collecting entropy.
If the initialization is successful, which implies that the
statistical tests indicate the underlying system is appropriate,
the call returns with
.IR 0 .
A return code other than
.IR 0
is one of the codes listed under
.B ERRORS
and indicates a failure where the calling application
.B MUST NOT
use the CPU Jitter Random Number Generator.
.LP
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
behaves identically to
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
except that it allows the caller to provide the
.IR osr
and
.IR flags
parameters. Pass the same values to every subsequent
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ():
the startup test validates exactly that configuration - the time source, the
oversampling rate its cut-offs derive from, the memory size and the
compliance mode. Its verdict is kept per time source only, so a collector
allocated with other values on the same time source is accepted on a startup
that never tested them.
When specifying an oversampling rate
.IR osr
different than the default, the startup test honors this value and adjusts
the self-test cut-off thresholds to the same values as used at runtime.
An
.I osr
above the supported maximum returns
.IR EPROGERR ,
as do the reserved
.I flags
described below and
.B JENT_DISABLE_MEMORY_ACCESS
in FIPS or NTG.1 mode, all of which every collector allocation refuses.
.LP
.BR jent_selftest ()
runs the known answer tests of the conditioning component: SHA3-256 on a
single and on a multi-block message, SHAKE-256 and XDRBG-256. These are the
tests
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
performs before any other. They are offered separately for callers that
like to have to repeat them periodically over the lifetime of a long-running process,
or on demand. The call returns
.IR 0
on success and
.IR EHASH
on failure, the same code
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
reports for it. The tests run on stack-local state alone, allocate nothing and
never block; they may therefore be called from any thread at any time,
including in parallel with entropy collection and before initialization -
see THREAD SAFETY for the one exception.
The
.IR entropy_collector
argument binds the verdict to an instance: on failure, that instance is
permanently taken out of service and
.BR jent_read_entropy ()
and
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
return
.B JENT_ERR_SELFTEST
instead of data from then on, in every mode of operation, not only under
FIPS, and its status document reports
.I selftestFailed
as
.IR true .
It may be
.I NULL
to obtain the verdict without binding it to an instance.
.LP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ()
allocates a CPU Jitter entropy collector instance and returns the handle
to the caller. If the allocation fails, including memory
constraints, the call returns
.IR NULL .
The function requires two arguments, the oversampling rate
.IR osr
and a set of flags with
.IR flags .
The
.IR osr
value defines the amount of oversampling performed by the entropy
collector. Usually, a caller wants to provide the value 0 here to
apply the default oversampling. The call raises any value below 3 to 3 automatically, and so does
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ().
A value above 20 is refused with a NULL return instead.
These bounds are the library's internal
.B JENT_MIN_OSR
and
.BR JENT_MAX_OSR ,
which
.I jitterentropy.h
does not export.
.LP
The
.IR flags
value is either zero or one or more of the following flags.
.TP
.B JENT_DISABLE_MEMORY_ACCESS
If the system is constrained with memory, this flag
disables the allocation of that memory and therefore memory accesses. But
that also implies that the entropy collection process only relies on the
complexity of the CPU. Note, if somebody knows all details of that CPU
complexity, that person may potentially reduce the entropy delivered by the CPU
complexity. If that person can push the generated entropy below a threshold,
the CPU Jitter random number generator starts overestimating entropy from the
noise source. Thus, disabling memory accesses and relying only on the CPU
complexity should only be done if you really know what you are doing.
.TP
.B JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER
This flag can be used to force the Jitter RNG to use the internal
high-resolution timer instead of using the hardware time stamp. Without it,
an allocation uses the hardware timer with precedence if a startup self test
passed on it, and falls back to the internal timer for that instance alone
otherwise. The startup self test of
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
and
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
measures the common divisor of the time deltas once per timer, which every
instance on that timer uses. If the internal timer is not compiled, requesting
this flag returns an error. The counting thread and its state belong to the
.IR entropy_collector
it was spawned for, so instances do not interfere with one another; this does
not make a single instance safe to use from two threads. See
.B THREAD SAFETY
below.
.TP
.B JENT_DISABLE_INTERNAL_TIMER
This flag can be used to ensure that the internal timer is not used.
If this flag is used together with
.B JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER
this is treated as an error and the allocation returns NULL. Also,
in case no startup self test passes on the time source, the allocation
returns NULL instead of falling back to the internal timer.
.TP
.B JENT_FORCE_FIPS
Force full FIPS 140 and SP800-90B compliance irrespective of the
FIPS setting of the underlying operating system.
.TP
.B JENT_NTG1
Enable the BSI AIS 20/31 v3.0 NTG.1 compliant behavior.
.TP
.B JENT_CACHE_ALL
Derive the memory size of the memory access noise source from the summed
size of all data caches rather than from the L1 data cache alone. Without a
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_*
flag, the size is the next power of two above that sum; with the default of
L1 alone it is four times the next power of two above the L1 size.
.TP
.BR JENT_DISABLE_STIR ", " JENT_DISABLE_UNBIAS
Accepted for compatibility and without effect.
.TP
.B JENT_FORCE_SECURE_MEM
Require the state of the entropy collector to be secure memory.
The Jitter RNG always asks for it - a memory lock from the operating
system, or an allocation from the secure memory arena of the crypto
library it was built against; this flag decides what happens when that
request cannot be granted. Without it - the default - the collector is
created regardless, at the price of a state that may be written to the
swap space. It is then treated as insecure memory: the
.I secureMemory
entry of
.BR jent_status ()
reports
.IR false .
With the flag the
allocation fails instead, which is what an environment not permitting
locking - a CI system or a restricted container with a too small
.IR RLIMIT_MEMLOCK ,
say - will run into, as will an application that has not configured the
secure memory arena of libgcrypt (GCRYCTL_INIT_SECMEM) or OpenSSL
.RB ( CRYPTO_secure_malloc_init ()),
each of which is process-wide state the library does not establish
itself.
.IP
The flag is implied by
.B JENT_NTG1
and
.BR JENT_FORCE_FIPS ,
whose compliance claims cover the protection of the collector state.
It has no effect in the Linux kernel, where the memory is secure by
construction. With AWS-LC the library locks the pages of the AWS-LC
allocation itself where the platform offers a memory lock; elsewhere AWS-LC
memory is wiped but not locked and never claims to be secure.
.IP
The memory access region, whose size
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_*
sets, is not part of that state and is never secure memory: it is what the
memory access noise source is timed on, and its contents are neither hashed
into the entropy pool nor part of any output. It is zeroed on allocation and
wiped on release, and excluded from core dumps where the platform allows, but
it is not locked and not taken from a secure memory arena. What a collector
locks is therefore a small fixed amount whatever its memory size: one page of
state, a second with the internal timer, and two more for the delta history
while its startup runs. A health test reallocation holds two collectors while
it runs. The small default quotas - the 64 KiB
.I RLIMIT_MEMLOCK
of older Linux systems and of Android apps, the 200 KiB minimum working set a
Windows process starts with, against which
.BR VirtualLock ()
is charged - hold several collectors at a time; the Windows default held 44.
An application allocating more raises the quotas itself, with
.BR setrlimit ()
or
.BR SetProcessWorkingSetSizeEx ();
.I tests/jitterentropy-memlock.h
does this for the tools shipped with the library and can be used as it stands.
The library does not do it itself: the limits are process-wide state,
extending them evicts what the host application reserved, and they cannot be
restored on free.
.TP
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_*
Set the size of the memory access region, which is then used as given.
Without one of these flags, the size is derived from the data caches: four
times the next power of two above the L1 data cache size, or with
.B JENT_CACHE_ALL
the next power of two above the sum of all data caches. Where no cache size
is discoverable, it is 256 KiB. On 32-bit targets a derived size is capped at
64 MiB. A derived size doubles with every reallocation after a health test
failure; one set by a flag does not.
The larger the memory, the more entropy is collected, yet the default is safe
on most CPUs. With memory pressure but a sufficient entropy rate a smaller
size may be used; with insufficient entropy a larger one.
The flags are
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_1kB " (1)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_2kB " (2)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_4kB " (3)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_8kB " (4)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_16kB " (5)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_32kB " (6)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_64kB " (7)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_128kB " (8)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_256kB " (9)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_512kB " (10)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_1MB " (11)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_2MB " (12)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_4MB " (13)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_8MB " (14)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_16MB " (15)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_32MB " (16)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_64MB " (17)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_128MB " (18)",
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_256MB " (19)",
and
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_512MB " (20)",
which is also
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_MAX ,
the index in parentheses. The size is 2 to the power of the index plus
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_OFFSET
(9) bytes.
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_TO_FLAGS ()
turns an index into its flag and
.BR JENT_FLAGS_TO_MAX_MEMSIZE ()
back, shifting by
.B JENT_FLAGS_TO_MEMSIZE_SHIFT
(27), and
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_MASK
selects the field in
.IR flags .
.TP
.B JENT_HASHLOOP_*
Set the number of hash loop iterations per measurement:
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_1 " (1)",
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_2 " (2)",
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_4 " (3)",
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_8 " (4)",
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_16 " (5)",
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_32 " (6)",
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_64 " (7)",
and
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_128 " (8)",
which is also
.BR JENT_MAX_HASHLOOP ,
the index in parentheses: index
.I n
selects 2^(\fIn\fR\-1) iterations.
Without one of these flags - index 0 - the built-in default is used, one
iteration unless the build sets a different one with
.BR JENT_HASH_LOOP_DEFAULT .
Every reallocation after a health test failure doubles the count, up to the
maximum.
.BR JENT_HASHLOOP_TO_FLAGS ()
turns an index into its flag and
.BR JENT_FLAGS_TO_HASHLOOP ()
back, shifting by
.B JENT_FLAGS_TO_HASHLOOP_SHIFT
(23), and
.B JENT_MAX_HASHLOOP_MASK
selects the field in
.IR flags .
.LP
Bits 9 to 22 of
.I flags
are reserved, as are memory size field values above
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_MAX
and hash loop field values above
.BR JENT_MAX_HASHLOOP .
Flags using them are refused rather than ignored:
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
returns
.I EPROGERR
and
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ()
NULL. A flag a later version defines is therefore not silently taken by one
that does not know what it asks for.
.LP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_free ()
zeroizes and frees the given CPU Jitter entropy collector instance.
.LP
.BR jent_read_entropy ()
generates a random bit stream and returns it to the caller.
.IR entropy_collector
is the CPU Jitter entropy collector instance which shall be used
to obtain random numbers.
.IR data
is the destination memory location where the random bit stream
is written to. The memory must have already been allocated by the
caller.
.IR len
is a length value provided by the caller indicating the number
of bytes the CPU Jitter Random Number Generator shall generate.
The caller can provide any value greater than 0. The caller
must ensure that
.IR data
is at least as big as
.IR len
indicates. The function returns the number of bytes generated
when the request is successfully completed. In case of an error, one of
the negative error codes listed under
.B ERRORS
is returned.
.LP
Health test failures are reported only in FIPS or NTG.1 mode. Outside them,
.BR jent_read_entropy ()
never returns an RCT, APT, lag or RCT-with-memory error, except
.B JENT_ERR_RCT_PERMANENT
for a noise source that stopped delivering; see
.BR ERRORS .
.LP
When a health test failure is reported, the Jitter RNG will not
have any data provided in
.IR data .
The entropy collector instance will remain in error state. To recover,
the entropy collector instance
.B MUST
be deallocated and a fresh instance must be allocated. It is
recommended that you increase the
.IR osr
value at least by one when newly allocating the Jitter RNG with
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ()
which implies that the health tests are less sensitive due to the
fact that the assumed entropy rate of the noise source is lower.
.LP
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
is a service function to and therefore operates identically to
.BR jent_read_entropy ()
with the exception that it automatically re-allocates the entropy collector
if an intermittent health test failure is reported, which happens in FIPS or
NTG.1 mode only. Before reallocation, a
new power-on health test is performed. The allocation of the new entropy
collector automatically increases the OSR by one. This is done based
on the idea that a health test failure indicates that the assumed
entropy rate is too high.
.LP
Where the noise source does not let it recover - the OSR would exceed its
maximum, or the replacement fails its own startup - the collector is out of
service for good and the failure is returned as a permanent one: the
permanent failure the replacement's startup ended in, or else the
.B *_PERMANENT
counterpart of the intermittent failure, which later calls then report as
well. Where the platform cannot provide the replacement - no memory, or no
counting thread for the internal timer - the intermittent failure is returned
and the collector is left as it was, and the next call tries again; that is
the only intermittent failure it returns. A permanent failure during the startup of a replacement ends the
recovery at once rather than moving on to the next OSR. If an error is
returned by this function, the Jitter RNG is not safe to be used on the
current system.
.LP
Otherwise the function
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
has the same error codes as
.BR jent_read_entropy ().
.LP
.BR jent_status ()
writes a JSON document describing an entropy collector into
.IR buf :
the library version, the identifier of the instance, what it has delivered
over its lifetime, the state of each health test and of a bound self test,
the runtime environment it
found and the configuration it is running with. It is what the
.B FIPS 140-3 Considerations
and
.B AIS 20/31 NTG.1 Compliance
sections below ask to be read for the properties they require. The document
is a serialization of state the instance holds, not a measurement: it costs
no entropy and collects nothing.
.LP
At most
.IR buflen
bytes are written, the terminating NUL included, and the result is always NUL
terminated. The call returns
.IR 0
when the whole document fit and
.IR \-1
when it did not - a document cut short is not handed back as if it were
complete, because a consumer would fail to parse it - as well as for a
.IR buf
of
.IR NULL
or a
.IR buflen
of zero. A buffer of 4096 bytes holds the document; that is the size the
kernel module's interfaces use for it.
.LP
The
.IR entropy_collector
may be
.IR NULL .
The result is then a complete JSON object carrying the library version alone,
which is the way to ask for the version without allocating an instance; it is
not an error and returns
.IR 0 .
.LP
.BR jent_uuid ()
copies the identifier of an instance - an RFC 9562 version 4 or 8 UUID in its
canonical string form - into
.IR buf ,
which must hold
.B JENT_UUID_STRLEN
bytes, the terminating NUL included. That length is a fixed property of the
format, not a tunable, and is not to be defined by the application. The identifier is assigned once, when the
instance is allocated, and is stable for its lifetime: it is carried over when
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
reallocates the collector on a health test failure, so it names the instance
rather than the allocation. It is the
.IR uuid
field of the status document above.
.LP
The call returns
.IR 0
on success and
.IR \-1
on error, which includes a NULL
.IR entropy_collector
and a
.IR buf
too small for the string. Where the platform offers no cryptographic random
number generator to draw an identifier from, it is a version 8 UUID hashed with
SHA3-256 from a process-wide counter and the current time.
.LP
.BR jent_secure_memory_supported ()
reports whether this build has a mechanism for secure memory at all - a memory
lock from the operating system, or the secure arena of the crypto library the
library was built against. It describes the build, not an allocation: whether a
given entropy collector actually holds such memory is what
.B JENT_FORCE_SECURE_MEM
makes a condition of, and what the
.IR secureMemory
field of the status document reports. It returns
.IR 1
where there is such a mechanism and
.IR 0
where there is none.
.LP
.BR JENT_MAJVERSION
indicates API / ABI incompatible changes, functional changes that require
consumer to be updated.
.LP
.BR JENT_MINVERSION
indicates API compatible, ABI may change, functional enhancements only,
consumer can be left unchanged if enhancements are not considered.
.LP
.BR JENT_PATCHLEVEL
indicates API / ABI compatible, no functional changes, no enhancements, bug
fixes only. Also, the entropy collection is not changed in any way that
would necessitate a re-assessment.
.LP
.BR JENT_VERSION
the version number of the library as an integer value that is monotonically
increasing. The version numbers are multiples of 100. For example, version
1.2.3 is converted to 1020300 -- the last two digits are reserved for
future use.
.LP
.BR jent_version ()
returns
.BR JENT_VERSION .
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR jent_entropy_init (),
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
and
.BR jent_selftest ()
return 0 on success and a positive error code otherwise.
.PP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ()
returns the new collector, or NULL on failure.
.BR jent_entropy_collector_free ()
and
.BR jent_notime_fini ()
return no value.
.PP
.BR jent_read_entropy ()
and
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
return the number of bytes generated,
.IR len ,
on success and a negative error code otherwise.
.PP
.BR jent_status ()
returns 0 on success and \-1 when
.I buf
is NULL or too small.
.BR jent_uuid ()
also returns \-1 for a NULL
.IR entropy_collector .
.PP
.BR jent_secure_memory_supported ()
returns 1 where the build has a mechanism for secure memory and 0 where it
has none.
.BR jent_version ()
returns the
.B JENT_VERSION
of the library, which may differ from the one of the header an application
was compiled against.
.PP
.BR jent_set_fips_failure_callback (),
.BR jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl ()
and
.BR jent_entropy_set_notime_cpu ()
return 0 on success,
.I \-EAGAIN
once an initialization has run, and the last two
.I \-EOPNOTSUPP
without the internal timer.
.BR jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl ()
returns
.I \-EINVAL
for a NULL handler or one with a NULL callback.
.BR jent_notime_init ()
returns 0 on success,
.I \-ENOENT
on a system with fewer than two CPUs,
.I \-ENOMEM
when its context cannot be allocated, and any negative errno the CPU count
query fails with. Without the internal timer it is a stub returning 0.
.SH ERRORS
.BR jent_entropy_init (),
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
and
.BR jent_selftest ()
return 0 on success or one of the following positive codes.
.TP
.BR ENOTIME " (1)"
No timer service is available.
.TP
.BR ECOARSETIME " (2)"
The timer is too coarse for the RNG.
.TP
.BR ENOMONOTONIC " (3)"
The timer is not monotonically increasing, or no reading reached the common
timer divisor analysis.
.TP
.BR EMINVARIATION " (4)"
Unused.
.TP
.BR EVARVAR " (5)"
Unused.
.TP
.BR EMINVARVAR " (6)"
The variations of the timer variations are too small.
.TP
.BR EPROGERR " (7)"
Invalid argument: an
.I osr
above the supported maximum, a reserved flag bit (9 to 22), a memory size
field above
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_MAX ,
a hash loop field above
.BR JENT_MAX_HASHLOOP ,
or
.B JENT_DISABLE_MEMORY_ACCESS
in FIPS or NTG.1 mode.
.TP
.BR ESTUCK " (8)"
Too many stuck measurements during the startup.
.TP
.BR EHEALTH " (9)"
A health test failed during the startup.
.TP
.BR ERCT " (10)"
The repetition count test failed during the startup.
.TP
.BR EHASH " (11)"
A known answer test of the conditioning component failed: SHA3-256 (single-
or multi-block), SHAKE-256 or XDRBG-256.
.TP
.BR EMEM " (12)"
Memory for the startup could not be allocated.
.TP
.BR EGCD " (13)"
The GCD self test failed.
.PP
.BR jent_read_entropy ()
and
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
return the number of bytes generated on success or one of the following
negative codes. The health test codes are returned in FIPS or NTG.1 mode only,
except
.B JENT_ERR_RCT_PERMANENT
for a stopped noise source.
Of the health test codes,
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
returns only the permanent ones.
.TP
.BR JENT_ERR_EINVAL " (\-1)"
No entropy collector, or no data buffer for a non-zero length.
.TP
.BR JENT_ERR_RCT " (\-2)"
The SP800-90B repetition count test failed.
.TP
.BR JENT_ERR_APT " (\-3)"
The SP800-90B adaptive proportion test failed.
.TP
.BR JENT_ERR_NOTIME " (\-4)"
The internal timer cannot be initialized.
.TP
.BR JENT_ERR_LAG " (\-5)"
The lag predictor test failed.
.TP
.BR JENT_ERR_RCT_PERMANENT " (\-6)"
The repetition count test failed permanently. It is also what a noise source
that stopped delivering usable measurements is reported as, in every mode of
operation: the collection loop gives up after a run of rejected measurements
no working source produces, so a stopped clock ends the call instead of being
waited on forever.
.TP
.BR JENT_ERR_APT_PERMANENT " (\-7)"
The adaptive proportion test failed permanently.
.TP
.BR JENT_ERR_LAG_PERMANENT " (\-8)"
The lag predictor test failed permanently.
.TP
.BR JENT_ERR_RCT_MEM " (\-9)"
The repetition count test with memory failed.
.TP
.BR JENT_ERR_RCT_MEM_PERMANENT " (\-10)"
The repetition count test with memory failed permanently.
.TP
.BR JENT_ERR_SELFTEST " (\-11)"
A
.BR jent_selftest ()
run bound to this instance failed; the failure is permanent, and
.BR jent_status ()
reports
.I selftestFailed
as
.IR true .
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
See
.B THREAD SAFETY
below for the details.
.ad l
.TS
allbox;
lbx lb
lx l.
Interface	Thread safety
T{
\%\fBjent_entropy_init\fP(), \%\fBjent_entropy_init_ex\fP(), \%\fBjent_entropy_collector_alloc\fP(), \%\fBjent_selftest\fP(), \%\fBjent_secure_memory_supported\fP(), \%\fBjent_version\fP()
T}	MT-Safe
T{
\%\fBjent_read_entropy\fP(), \%\fBjent_read_entropy_safe\fP(), \%\fBjent_status\fP(), \%\fBjent_uuid\fP(), \%\fBjent_entropy_collector_free\fP()
T}	MT-Safe race:entropy_collector
T{
\%\fBjent_set_fips_failure_callback\fP(), \%\fBjent_entropy_switch_notime_impl\fP(), \%\fBjent_entropy_set_notime_cpu\fP()
T}	MT-Unsafe init
T{
\%\fBjent_notime_init\fP(), \%\fBjent_notime_fini\fP()
T}	MT-Safe race:ctx
.TE
.ad
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The library reads no environment variables.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc/sys/crypto/fips_enabled
Enables the FIPS mode on Linux when it reads 1, unless the library was built
against an external crypto library, whose FIPS mode is asked instead.
.TP
.I /sys/devices/system/cpu
The data cache sizes, which the memory access noise source is sized from, and
the online CPUs on Linux.
.TP
.I /proc/cpuinfo
The core types, whose largest caches stand in on Arm systems that report
none.
.TP
.I /dev/urandom
The random identifier of an instance, on Linux where
.BR getrandom (2)
is not available and on other Unix systems without
.BR arc4random (3).
The BSDs and macOS use
.BR arc4random (3),
Windows
.BR BCryptGenRandom ().
.SH STANDARDS
The noise source and its health tests follow NIST SP 800-90B: the repetition
count test and the adaptive proportion test, extended by a repetition count
test with memory and a lag predictor test. The output is conditioned with
SHAKE-256 of FIPS 202 into an XDRBG-256. The NTG.1 mode follows the
functionality class NTG.1 of the BSI AIS 20/31 v3.0. The instance identifier
is an RFC 9562 UUID.
.SH HISTORY
.BR jent_entropy_init (),
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc (),
.BR jent_entropy_collector_free (),
.BR jent_read_entropy ()
and
.BR jent_version ()
are available since 2.1.0 or earlier;
.BR jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl (),
.BR jent_notime_init ()
and
.BR jent_notime_fini ()
since 3.1.0;
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
and
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
since 3.2.0;
.BR jent_set_fips_failure_callback ()
since 3.4.0;
.BR jent_status ()
and
.BR jent_secure_memory_supported ()
since 3.7.0;
.BR jent_selftest (),
.BR jent_uuid ()
and
.BR jent_entropy_set_notime_cpu ()
since 3.7.1.
.PP
.B JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER
is available since 3.0.0,
.B JENT_FORCE_FIPS
and
.B JENT_DISABLE_INTERNAL_TIMER
since 3.0.2,
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_32kB
to
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_512MB
since 3.3.0, renumbered in 3.7.0, which added
.B JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_1kB
to
.BR JENT_MAX_MEMSIZE_16kB ,
.BR JENT_NTG1 ,
.B JENT_CACHE_ALL
and
.B JENT_HASHLOOP_*
- renumbered in 3.7.1, where
.B JENT_HASHLOOP_1
became one iteration rather than the default -,
.B JENT_FORCE_SECURE_MEM
and the
.B JENT_ERR_*
definitions since 3.7.1.
.SH FIPS 140-3 CONSIDERATIONS
In order for the Jitter RNG to execute compliant to FIPS 140-3 and by
extension also SP800-90B and SP800-90C compliant, the following
considerations must be applied:
.TP
.B Enable FIPS mode
The FIPS mode is enabled by using the
.IR JENT_FORCE_FIPS
flag during initialization of the Jitter RNG library. On Linux, the
FIPS mode is transparently enabled if the entire operating system
was booted in FIPS mode, usually by using the "fips=1" Linux kernel
command line parameter. On Windows, it is transparently enabled when the
system FIPS policy ("System cryptography: Use FIPS compliant algorithms")
is on, as
.BR BCryptGetFipsAlgorithmMode ()
reports it.
.TP
.B Perform heuristic entropy analysis
The test tool set provided as part of the Jitter RNG library source
distribution contains the helper to obtain raw noise data at runtime
as well as at initialization time to calculate the SP800-90B entropy rate.
This rate must be above 0.333, the implied heuristic minimum by the Jitter
RNG library.
.TP
.B Resolve insufficient entropy
If insufficient entropy is found during the aforementioned SP800-90B
analysis, the test tool set provides a helper to analyze optimal
settings - see the test tool set for raw entropy for details. The resulting
configuration values are expected to be used with the
.IR flags
parameter of the calls
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ()
and
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex () .
It is recommended that the function
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
API call is used for generating random numbers. The Jitter RNG must not be
used after a permanent health test failure.
.TP
.B Do not force the internal timer
.B JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER
must not be set.
.TP
.B Verify the status
.BR jent_status ()
must report
.I fipsMode
as
.IR true ,
.I internalTimer
and
.I selftestFailed
as
.IR false ,
and no failure in
.IR healthFailure .
.SH AIS 20/31 NTG.1 CONSIDERATIONS
In order for the Jitter RNG to execute compliant to the functionality class
NTG.1 of the German BSI's AIS 20/31 v3.0, the following considerations must
be applied:
.TP
.B Enable NTG.1 mode
The NTG.1 mode is enabled by using the
.IR JENT_NTG1
flag with
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
and every
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ().
It implies the FIPS mode with its health tests, where the adaptive
proportion test, the repetition count test and the repetition count test with
memory then carry an 8-fold safety margin in their cut-offs and the lag
predictor keeps the common ones, as well as
.B JENT_FORCE_SECURE_MEM
and
.BR JENT_DISABLE_INTERNAL_TIMER .
It may be combined with
.BR JENT_FORCE_FIPS .
.B JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER
is refused with
.IR ENOTIME
and a NULL allocation, and
.B JENT_DISABLE_MEMORY_ACCESS
with
.IR EPROGERR
and a NULL allocation: NTG.1 requires the hash loop and the memory access
as two noise sources, each of which is sampled on its own during the
startup of every collector.
.TP
.B Perform entropy analysis
Each of the two noise sources and their common operation must show an
entropy rate of 8/OSR or more, both in the SP800-90B runtime and restart
tests. The test tool set provided as part of the Jitter RNG library source
distribution records the three data sets and analyzes them - see the
NTG.1 recording and validation helpers of the test tool set for raw entropy.
.TP
.B Resolve insufficient entropy
If the rate is insufficient, a higher oversampling rate, memory size or hash
loop count may reach it; the test tool set helps finding them. The resulting
.IR osr
and
.IR flags
are expected to be used with
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
and
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ().
If the oversampling rate needed is above 20, the Jitter RNG cannot be used on
that system.
.TP
.B Verify the status
.BR jent_status ()
must report
.I ntg1Mode
as
.IR true ,
.I internalTimer
and
.I selftestFailed
as
.IR false ,
and a
.I memoryBlockSizeBytes
of at least four times the L1 data cache size.
.TP
.B Handle health test failures
It is recommended that
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
is used for generating random numbers. The Jitter RNG must not be used after
a permanent health test failure.
.SH THREAD SAFETY
The library takes no locks. What is safe concurrently is stated here; anything
not stated is the caller's to serialize.
.TP
.B An entropy collector belongs to one thread at a time
.BR jent_read_entropy (),
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
and
.BR jent_status ()
mutate or read the state of the
.IR entropy_collector
they are given - the conditioning state, the previous time stamp, the health
test counters - without any synchronization of their own. Two threads using
one collector at the same time corrupt that state, and the output is then
neither unpredictable nor necessarily distinct. Give each thread its own
collector, or hold a lock of your own across the call. Separate collectors are
independent and need no lock between them.
.TP
.B Configure before any concurrent use
.BR jent_entropy_set_notime_cpu (),
.BR jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl ()
and
.BR jent_set_fips_failure_callback ()
write process-wide state that the calls above only read. They must all be made
before the first
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
or
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
and before any thread generates. Once an initialization has run they return
.IR \-EAGAIN ,
which reports that the window has closed - it does not synchronize with a
thread already inside it. The window closes on the first attempt, not on the
first success: an initialization that fails leaves the three returning
.I \-EAGAIN
as well, so a caller that means to install its own timer thread for a second
attempt - after the built-in one was refused, say - has to install it before
the first.
.TP
.B Initialization may be called from several threads
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
and
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
publish what they establish - the self test verdict and the common timer
divisor, per timer - through atomic stores, so
several threads may run them at once and a thread told the state is
established also sees what was established. Running one of them once, before
any concurrent use, remains cheaper: each call measures the timer afresh.
.TP
.B jent_selftest() is reentrant
It runs on stack-local state alone, allocates nothing and never blocks, so it
may be called from any thread at any time, including in parallel with
.BR jent_read_entropy ()
on the very instance the verdict is bound to. The one exception is
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
on that instance: its recovery frees the collector and replaces it, and a
verdict bound to the freed pointer would be written into released memory.
Serialize the two for the same instance, or pass NULL and act on the return
value.
.SH NOTES
In addition to use the generated random bit stream directly
for cryptographic operations, the output of
.BR jent_read_entropy ()
or
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
can be used for seeding a deterministic random number generator.
.PP
Every call that runs the noise source clears the stack it ran over before it
returns, so that the time stamps, deltas and conditioning temporaries left in
those frames do not survive the call on a stack that is neither locked,
guard-paged nor kept out of a core dump. The wipe uses a buffer of its own,
which sets a floor under what these calls need:
.BR jent_entropy_init (),
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex (),
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc (),
.BR jent_read_entropy ()
and
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
descend some 4.5 kB in a hosted build, nearly all of it the wipe.
.PP
The wipe is not always the deepest of them, though.
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ()
draws the per-instance identifier from the operating system's CSPRNG once
the collector is allocated, before its startup runs the noise source, and
where the first such call in a process has machinery to stand up,
that draw goes deeper than anything the library itself does: on Windows
.BR BCryptGenRandom ()
brings up the system-preferred provider, and the first
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc ()
of a process descends some 6.8 kB where a later one takes 4.2 kB. A hosted
caller should therefore allow
.B 8 kB
for these five. That is about a kilobyte over the deepest reach measured
there, and the deepest is not the usual one: a few per cent of calls take a
path some 250 bytes further down, so the common figure is not the ceiling. A
caller invoking them on a thread whose stack it sizes itself has to provide
it. Kernel and freestanding builds do not scrub and do not need it, their
stacks already having the properties the wipe is there to make up for.
.PP
This C interface is not callable in the Linux kernel: the
.I jitter_rng
module of the source distribution exports no symbols and provides the Jitter
RNG through
.IR /dev/jitterentropy ,
the hardware random number generator framework, the crypto API,
.I /proc/jitterentropy
and ioctls instead; see
.I linux_kernel/README.md
there.
.SH EXAMPLES
The program below checks the platform, allocates a collector, reads 32 bytes
with the self-recovering call and prints the status document. Build it with
.IR "cc example.c -ljitterentropy" .
.PP
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <jitterentropy.h>

int main(void)
{
	struct rand_data *ec;
	char status[4096];
	unsigned char buf[32];
	ssize_t ret;
	int err;

	/* Once per process, before the first collector. */
	err = jent_entropy_init();
	if (err) {
		fprintf(stderr, "jent_entropy_init: %d\en", err);
		return 1;
	}

	ec = jent_entropy_collector_alloc(0, 0);
	if (!ec) {
		fprintf(stderr, "jent_entropy_collector_alloc failed\en");
		return 1;
	}

	/* Replaces ec with a new instance on an intermittent failure. */
	ret = jent_read_entropy_safe(&ec, (char *)buf, sizeof(buf));
	if (ret < 0) {
		fprintf(stderr, "jent_read_entropy_safe: %zd\en", ret);
		jent_entropy_collector_free(ec);
		return 1;
	}
	printf("%zd bytes read\en", ret);

	if (!jent_status(ec, status, sizeof(status)))
		puts(status);

	jent_entropy_collector_free(ec);
	return 0;
}
.EE
.PP
A caller with a configuration of its own starts with
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
and allocates every collector with the same
.I osr
and
.IR flags :
.PP
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <jitterentropy.h>

int main(void)
{
	/*
	 * One configuration for the startup test and every collector.
	 * An osr of 0 is promoted to the default minimum, 3
	 * (JENT_MIN_OSR, internal to the library).
	 */
	const unsigned int osr = 0;
	const unsigned int flags = JENT_CACHE_ALL;
	struct rand_data *ec;
	int err;

	err = jent_entropy_init_ex(osr, flags);
	if (err) {
		fprintf(stderr, "jent_entropy_init_ex: %d\en", err);
		return 1;
	}

	ec = jent_entropy_collector_alloc(osr, flags);
	if (!ec) {
		fprintf(stderr, "jent_entropy_collector_alloc failed\en");
		return 1;
	}

	/* ... read as in the first example ... */

	jent_entropy_collector_free(ec);
	return 0;
}
.EE
